{
  "title": "--sequential-games uses only the FIRST manifest config, so a batch of \
N games all play the same matchup",
  "description": "A launcher writes N per-game configs into a manifest and runs \
`--batch-config-manifest manifest.json --sequential-games N`. Every game in the \
batch is played with the FIRST config. The remaining N-1 are written, passed, \
parsed, and ignored. Nothing warns.\n\n\
MEASURED on a live 5,000-game corpus run (stopped because of this), 116 games \
completed, 5 games per worker:\n\
  - 30 distinct deck pairs over 116 games -> 3.9 games per pair\n\
  - pair repeat histogram {5: 18 groups, 4: 2, 3: 1, 2: 6, 1: 3}\n\
  - 24 of 24 multi-game config groups had ALL games on ONE deck pair\n\
  - 60 distinct decks touched, from a 952-deck training pool\n\n\
The games are NOT duplicates: seeds vary per game via MAGEBENCH_GAME_SEEDS, so \
shuffles differ. In one group the five games ran 133/160/149/236/274 decisions \
with different opening hands. So the corpus is valid and silently NARROW — which \
is why nothing downstream would have caught it. The rendered conversations look \
correct; only a count of distinct (deckA, deckB) pairs reveals it.\n\n\
Impact for us: intended 5,000 matchups, would have got ~1,000. Matchup diversity \
is the entire point of the corpus, so this is a p1.\n\n\
Repro: build a manifest of N configs with DIFFERENT decks, run with \
--sequential-games N, then compare `deck_path` across the produced \
game_meta.json files. Expected N distinct pairs; actual 1.\n\n\
Suggested shape of a fix: consume the manifest entry per sequential game, and \
fail loudly if the manifest length and --sequential-games disagree — a manifest \
that is silently truncated to its first element is worse than an error, because \
the run succeeds.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 1,
  "type": "bug",
  "labels": [
    "orchestration",
    "batch",
  ],
